\section{Setting up the diagnostic quiz, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the quizzes of \cref{prior-knowledge,end-measure}: it sets
them up in the learning platform, and it prepares their results for the
analysis.
Four files are tangled from this section: [[quiz-background.json]],
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas,
summarises them, and prepares the open-text answers for coding.

\subsection{The three quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-background.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
\end{minted}
There are three quizzes.
The background quiz and the start-of-course debugging quiz together
form this study's part of the diagnostic of \cref{prior-knowledge}
(the concept quizzes that measure prior conceptual knowledge are the
companion study's \autocite{VTProgMisconceptions}, run in the same
course); the end-of-course debugging quiz repeats the debugging
questions to measure the gain (\cref{end-measure}).
The debugging test is separate from the background quiz because the two
differ in kind: the debugging questions have right and wrong answers,
so that quiz can be graded---and the students may well want to know how
they did---whereas the background items have no right answers and
nothing to grade.
The debugging items are defined once and appear in both debugging
quizzes.
The quiz titles name debugging explicitly, since the companion study's
concept quizzes run in the same course and the two sets must stay
distinguishable for students and analysis alike.

<<[[quiz-background.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Diagnostic quiz: your programming background",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz maps your programming background so that we can adapt the course. It is not graded and there are no wrong answers. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 0,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<ungraded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<background items>>
  ]
}
@

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Debugging quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about finding and fixing errors in programs, so that we can adapt the course. The questions show Python code; if you have not programmed before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 8,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<start result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-course debugging quiz opens with a question of its own:
whether the student prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the gain measure of
\cref{end-measure}---a student who reviews the material the night
before measures preparation, not what the course left behind.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Debugging quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the debugging questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. Afterwards you will see your score and, for each question, the correct answer. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 8,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<end result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<knowledge-part items>>
  ]
}
@

All three quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single
attempt suffices, and answers are never shuffled (order is meaningful
in several items).
The shared settings come in two chunks---scheduling and interaction.
Canvas keeps two groups of settings in objects of their own,
[[multiple_attempts]] and [[result_view_settings]], and silently
ignores the same keys written flat; so the interaction chunk carries
the attempts object whole, and the result-view chunks below are each
a complete [[result_view_settings]] object.
(The sibling quizzes of the terminal module were deployed with the
keys flat, and reading the settings back from Canvas is what caught
it: the result view came back empty, that is, Canvas's defaults, which
would have shown the correct answers after the pre-test.)
Every brace still opens and closes in the chunk that writes it: the
[[quiz_settings]] object opens and closes in the quiz descriptions
above.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
@

<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "none",
"allow_backtracking": true,
"multiple_attempts": {"multiple_attempts_enabled": false},
@

The quizzes differ in what the result view reveals.
The background quiz has nothing to grade, so it shows no points and no
correctness.
The start-of-course debugging quiz shows the points awarded---how well
the student did---but neither per-item correctness nor the correct
answers: the same questions must measure the gain at the end of the
course (\cref{end-measure}), so they have to stay fresh.

The end-of-course quiz has nothing left to protect, and the students
have earned the answers: it shows, item by item, whether the answer was
right and which alternative was, so that the last diagnostic teaches as
well as measures---the distractor a student chose is the misconception
they can now read against the correct one.
It also shows, under each item, a short text of ours saying why the
keyed alternative is right and what each distractor assumes
(\cref{app:debugging-items}).
That text travels with the item, and the two debugging quizzes share
their items, so the start quiz carries it too; the last key of each
object below is what hides it everywhere but at the end, just as the
two correctness keys above it hide the platform's own verdict, and all
three are worth reading back from Canvas like the rest of the settings.

<<ungraded result view>>=
"result_view_settings": {
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": false,
  "display_points_possible": false,
  "display_item_feedback": false
}
