\subsection{The background items}
\label{app:background-items}

The background quiz opens with the consent item---an arrangement kept
from the course's earlier diagnostic tests, which these quizzes
replace:
participation in the study is opt-in (\cref{ethics}), so we ask before
collecting anything.
Then follow the background items motivated in
\cref{prior-knowledge}: four about the student's programming, then six
about the schools the student went to.
The item titles double as the keys under which Canvas reports the
answers, so the analysis program (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) refers to
the items by these titles.
Positions are counted within a quiz, so the eleven here have nothing to
do with the debugging items numbered from eleven below
(\cref{app:debugging-items}): no quiz holds both.

<<background items>>=
<<item: consent>>,
<<item: previous programming>>,
<<item: how learned>>,
<<item: confidence>>,
<<item: debugging exercise>>,
<<item: school in years 1-3>>,
<<item: school in years 4-6>>,
<<item: school in years 7-9>>,
<<item: upper secondary school>>,
<<item: upper secondary programme>>,
<<item: preparatory year>>
@

The wording is the fuller one of the ethics assessment (\cref{ethics}):
it names every source the study uses---the quizzes and questionnaires,
the submissions, the programming logs and the platform activity---so
that the consent covers what is actually collected, and it links the
participant information page, which describes what learnlog records and
when, so that the consent is informed in the GDPR's sense.
The companion study's course-level concept quiz opens with the same
item, so a student who has answered one recognises the next.

