\subsection{The debugging items}
\label{app:debugging-items}

The questions of the debugging test are written for this study and
published here in full.
They follow a debugging episode from beginning to end---making the
failure reproducible, reading what the crash reports, locating and
explaining the fault, and verifying the fix---the process the expert
literature prescribes \autocite{WhyProgramsFail}; each question tests
one step of it, each distractor expresses a common way of skipping or
botching that step, and all weigh one point: we measure knowledge by
counting correct answers.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do
not point out the relevant aspects---discerning them is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}; the design is motivated question by question in
\cref{prior-knowledge}.

Each item is followed by the text the end-of-course quiz shows under
it: a short account of why the keyed alternative is right and what each
distractor assumes---the distractor a student chose is the
misconception they can now read against it, so that the last of the two
measurements also teaches.
Every item of the debugging test is closed, so this is the only kind of
answer there is to give here; the open accounts of the study sit in the
ungraded background quiz (\cref{app:background-items}), which shows no
feedback at all.
The text may name the aspect the item tests, since it is shown only
after the answer is submitted and only at the end of the course: the
start quiz hides all feedback, which it must, since the two quizzes
share these items and the questions have to stay fresh.
It paraphrases the rationale given before the item, and the two must be
kept in step.
A third guard keeps the text out of the data: neither the human coder
nor the pre-coding model is ever shown it (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}),
since a coder who had our expectation in front of them would be
confirming it rather than finding it.
What each distractor assumes is, for now, anticipated rather than
observed---no cohort has answered these questions yet---so the first
cohort's distractor frequencies are what will revise these texts.
