\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas compiles the responses into a student-analysis report: a CSV
file with one row per student and a block of columns per item, which
the program re-keys by the item titles above
(\cref{app:reading-the-report}).
The analysis program fetches that report itself---we know the course
and the quiz titles, so nothing needs to be clicked or downloaded by
hand---using the same [[canvaslms]] machinery as the command-line tool.
It then tabulates the closed items and writes the open-text answers to
a coding sheet: a CSV file with one row per student and columns for the
codes.
We code all the data we asked for, so that every collected answer is
usable: from the languages answer the languages themselves, roughly how
much they were used, and whether Python is among them; from the
debugging exercise whether the described steps exhibit contrast,
generalisation or fusion thinking (\cref{analysis,mq:agreement}); and
from the school answers the type of each school, which is looked up
rather than judged (\cref{app:background-items}).

The coding is done by a human, but a large language model prepares it:
with [[--llm]] the program asks a model (via the [[llm]] package) to
suggest the codes for each answer, and the human coder verifies or
overrides them in the sheet.
The suggested codes are kept in separate columns from the verified
ones, so the sheet always shows which is which.
The per-item texts of \cref{app:debugging-items} are for the student
and never enter this program: the coding sheet and the pre-coding
prompt carry the students' own answers alone, so what we expect an
answer to show is put in front of neither the coder nor the model.

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
"""Fetch and summarise the quiz results, and prepare the coding sheet.

The quizzes are defined in quiz-background.json and the two
quiz-knowledge-*.json files; all four files are tangled from the same
literate source (the appendix of the paper).  The results come straight
from Canvas via canvaslms; the report's item columns are re-keyed by
the item titles.  Only rows of students who answered yes to the consent
item of the background quiz are kept.

Requires canvaslms (and its configured credentials); the --llm option
additionally requires the llm package with a configured model.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import csv
import html
import io
import json
import os
import re
import statistics
import sys

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>
<<read the report by item title>>
<<fetch the results from Canvas>>
<<attach the login IDs>>
<<the consent filter>>
<<tabulate the closed items>>
<<pre-code the open answers with an LLM>>
<<write the coding sheet>>
<<analysis main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

The course name and the item titles are the join keys between the
tangled files: if a title changes in a quiz, this dictionary is the
only other place to change---and they live in the same source, side by
side.
The report does not name its columns by the titles at all; the reader
of \cref{app:reading-the-report} re-keys every row by title, so that
everything after it can look an item up by its title alone.

The school answers are open text as well, and they go on the coding
sheet with the rest, since the sheet is where the coder has them to
look up; but they are not among the answers the model pre-codes, as
which huvudman ran a school is settled by the register rather than by a
judgement anything could suggest.
Hence two lists where one would do: the prompt below is built from
[[CODED_OPEN_ITEMS]], the sheet from [[OPEN_ITEMS]].

What the register answers with goes in a column of its own, one per
stage, laid out exactly as the model's codes are: the lookup of
\cref{app:school-lookup} writes [[suggested_huvudman: lågstadiet]] and
its three siblings, and the coder writes the verified value in the bare
[[huvudman: lågstadiet]] beside it.
The four stage names are written down once and the rest is derived from
them---the item titles Canvas reports under, the coder's columns and
the register lookup's own table of school years---because these have to
agree, and three lists that must agree are three chances to disagree.

