\subsubsection{Reading the report}
\label{app:reading-the-report}

The student-analysis report is not the table the item titles suggest.
Canvas writes one row per student---the [[Name]] and [[ID]], the
sections, the submission time, the elapsed time and the attempt
number---and then, for every item in position order, a block of five
columns: [[ItemID]], [[ItemType]], a column named by the item's
\emph{body text}, the points earned, and a status such as
\enquote{Graded} or \enquote{No Answer}.
Two things follow.
The titles occur nowhere in the report, so the blocks have to be
matched to the items through their bodies, which the tangled quiz
descriptions above provide; and the four fixed headers repeat once per
item, so the report cannot be read with [[csv.DictReader]] at all,
since a dictionary keeps only the last of the columns that share a
name---it has to be read positionally.

Canvas flattens the HTML body into that column name without putting a
space where a block element ended, so the last word of one paragraph
and the first word of the next arrive joined into one.
Normalising the whitespace cannot repair that, because the space was
never written; discarding whitespace altogether can, because both
sides then lose the same thing.
The body alone would still not be a safe key---two items may share a
body and differ only in how they are answered---so the item type joins
it, and the pair is unique for every item of every quiz here.

The two sides do not arrive equally marked up either: the description
is HTML, the column is the text Canvas made of it, with the tags gone
and the entities resolved.
So the tags are stripped from the description alone, and then both
sides resolve entities and drop whitespace.
Stripping tags from the column too would be wrong, not merely
redundant: the tracebacks of \cref{app:debugging-items} are full of
[[&lt;module&gt;]], which the column shows as the bracket itself, and
the tag pattern would swallow it on that side only, so the item would
never match.

<<read the report by item title>>=
TAGS = re.compile(r"<[^>]+>")
SPACES = re.compile(r"\s+")


def item_key(kind, text):
    """Return the key under which a report block matches an item.

    The item type and the text stripped of all whitespace, since Canvas
    drops the spaces between block elements when it flattens the body
    into a column name; entities are resolved so that a description and
    a column that write a character differently still agree.
    """
    return kind, SPACES.sub("", html.unescape(text))
@

The bodies to match against come from the quiz descriptions tangled
above (\cref{app:background-items,app:debugging-items}), which is the
point of reading them here rather than writing the bodies down a
second time: an item whose wording is edited is edited in one place,
and the report can never be re-keyed by a title Canvas was never asked
about.
The items are sorted by position because the report's block order is
the item order, which is what the fallback below leans on.
Discarding the whitespace costs a little of what tells two items
apart, so the map is asked whether it still has one key per item: a
pair that collapsed into one would quietly hand both blocks the same
title, which is the only way this can go wrong without a block failing
to match at all.

<<read the report by item title>>=
def item_titles(path):
    """Return the item titles of a quiz description file.

    A pair: the map from item key (see item_key) to title, and the
    titles in position order, the fallback when a block matches none.
    """
    with open(path) as infile:
        items = sorted(json.load(infile)["items"],
                       key=lambda item: item["position"])
    order = [item["entry"]["title"] for item in items]
    keys = {item_key(item["entry"]["interaction_type_slug"],
                     TAGS.sub(" ", item["entry"]["item_body"])): title
            for item, title in zip(items, order)}
    if len(keys) < len(order):
        print(f"warning: two items of {os.path.basename(path)} share a"
              f" body and a type; their blocks cannot be told apart")
    return keys, order
@

A report is read with the description of the quiz it came from, and
the program is asked for a quiz by the same key [[QUIZ_TITLES]] uses,
so one function turns that key into the titles and every caller is
spared the join.
It looks the file up beside the program rather than in the working
directory: [[make programs]] tangles the descriptions and the program
into one place, but a saved report can be analysed from anywhere.

<<read the report by item title>>=
QUIZ_DIRECTORY = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))


def titles_of(quiz):
    """Return the item titles of the background, start or end quiz."""
    return item_titles(os.path.join(QUIZ_DIRECTORY, QUIZ_FILES[quiz]))
@

A block whose body matches no item is not a reason to stop: a report
older than the description, or an item last edited in Canvas rather
than here, would otherwise cost us the entire analysis.
Since the blocks come in item order, position answers where the body
does not---but it answers by assumption, so the program says which
title it took, and a mismatch stays visible instead of being absorbed
into a plausible-looking table.
The type of a block is not in the header but in the rows, where every
row repeats it, so a block is named from the first row of data.

<<read the report by item title>>=
def block_title(index, kind, body, titles):
    """Return the title of the item behind the index-th block."""
    keys, order = titles
    title = keys.get(item_key(kind, body))
    if title is None:
        title = order[index] if index < len(order) else body
        print(f"warning: report column {body[:40]!r}... matches no item"
              f" body; taken as {title!r} by position")
    return title
@

An item nobody reached earns an empty cell rather than a zero, and a
report Canvas has not finished grading can leave a cell that is not a
number at all.
Neither contributed anything to the score, so the conversion answers
with nothing rather than raising: a score is a summary, and refusing
to compute one because a single student stopped halfway would lose the
rest of the cohort with it.

<<read the report by item title>>=
def points(cell):
    """Return the points a block's cell records, 0 for an empty one."""
    try:
        return float(cell)
    except ValueError:
        return 0.0
@

Reading the report is then two steps: settle where the blocks are and
what each of them is called, which the header and the first row of
data answer once for the whole file, and rewrite every row against
that.
The block is always five columns in the same order, so the layout is
written down once and read out by name.
The text goes to the CSV reader as a stream rather than split into
lines first, so that a line break inside a quoted answer reaches the
reader as it was written.

<<read the report by item title>>=
BLOCK_WIDTH = 5
BLOCK_TYPE, BLOCK_BODY, BLOCK_EARNED = 1, 2, 3
SCORE_COLUMN = "score"


def read_report(text, titles):
    """Return the rows of a student-analysis report, keyed by title.

    The student columns keep their names; each item block becomes one
    column, the answer under the item's title; the points earned are
    summed into a "score" column.
    """
    reader = csv.reader(io.StringIO(text, newline=""))
    header = next(reader, None)
    data = [values for values in reader if any(values)]
    if not header or not data:
        return []
    <<locate the item blocks in the header>>
    <<re-key each row by item title>>
@

The header is what says where the blocks are: [[ItemID]] opens every
one of them and occurs nowhere else, so the columns each block spans
follow from its first, and every column outside them is a student
column that keeps the name Canvas gave it.

<<locate the item blocks in the header>>=
starts = [i for i, name in enumerate(header) if name == "ItemID"]
student = [i for i in range(len(header))
           if not any(start <= i < start + BLOCK_WIDTH
                      for start in starts)]
blocks = [(start, block_title(index, data[0][start + BLOCK_TYPE],
                              header[start + BLOCK_BODY], titles))
          for index, start in enumerate(starts)]
@

A row can be shorter than the header, since Canvas drops the trailing
cells of a student who stopped answering, so it is padded before
anything is read out of it---which is also what turns those items into
the empty answers [[points]] expects rather than an index error.
The points go under the name the score summary looks for, so the
summary needs to know nothing about blocks.

<<re-key each row by item title>>=
rows = []
for values in data:
    values = values + [""] * (len(header) - len(values))
    row = {header[i]: values[i] for i in student}
    row[SCORE_COLUMN] = str(sum(points(values[start + BLOCK_EARNED])
                                for start, _ in blocks))
    for start, title in blocks:
        row[title] = values[start + BLOCK_BODY]
    rows.append(row)
return rows
