\subsubsection{Fetching the report from Canvas}

Just as we create the quizzes with [[canvaslms]], we extract the
results with it: the same package provides the credentials, the course
lookup and the report machinery, so the program reuses them instead of
reimplementing the Canvas API calls.
The report is generated on demand by Canvas, so we request it, wait for
it, and download the CSV it points to.
Canvas serves that file as UTF-8 without saying so, and [[requests]]
then guesses Latin-1, which turns every Swedish letter in the students'
own words into two wrong characters; decoding the bytes ourselves is
what removes the guess.
The fetcher takes the quiz title and that quiz's item titles, so the
survey program of \cref{app:rspq} can reuse it for its administrations,
which are quizzes of their own; [[fetch_results]] merely looks both up
for the three quizzes here.

<<fetch the results from Canvas>>=
def fetch_results(which, titles):
    """Download the rows of the background, start or end quiz."""
    return fetch_report(QUIZ_TITLES[which], titles)


def fetch_report(title, titles):
    """Download the student-analysis rows for the quiz with this title.

    The rows come back keyed by item title (see read_report), so the
    titles must be those of this same quiz.
    """
    import appdirs
    import requests
    import canvaslms.cli
    import canvaslms.cli.login
    from canvasapi import Canvas
    from canvaslms.cli.quizzes import (
        create_new_quiz_report,
        filter_quizzes,
        is_new_quiz,
        poll_progress,
    )

    config_file = os.path.join(
        appdirs.user_config_dir("canvaslms"), "config.json"
    )
    config = canvaslms.cli.read_configuration(config_file)
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    if not hostname.startswith("http"):
        hostname = f"https://{hostname}"
    canvas = Canvas(hostname, token)

    course = next(
        c for c in canvas.get_courses()
        if COURSE in (c.name or "") or COURSE in (c.course_code or "")
    )
    quiz = next(filter_quizzes([course], title))
    if not is_new_quiz(quiz):
        sys.exit(f"quiz {quiz.title!r} is not a New Quiz")

    progress = poll_progress(
        create_new_quiz_report(course, quiz.id, canvas._Canvas__requester)
    )
    url = progress.results.get("url") if progress else None
    if not url:
        sys.exit("Canvas did not produce the student-analysis report")
    response = requests.get(url)
    response.raise_for_status()
    rows = read_report(response.content.decode("utf-8-sig"), titles)
    attach_logins(rows, course)
    return rows
@

\subsubsection{Finding the login ID of a row}
\label{app:login-lookup}

The report names its students and, in its [[ID]] column, identifies
them by the platform's number---and by the institution's, should a
report carry a [[sis_id]] column as well; the login ID itself lives in
the course roster, one API call away.
So each row is looked up in the roster by the identifier it does carry:
the platform ID first, since Canvas assigns it and it is the one the
report is built from, then the institutional ID, and only for a report
carrying neither does the name come into it---accepted then, but only
when exactly one enrolled student bears it.
A name borne by two is reported and left alone, so the two rows keep
the fallback of \cref{app:student-identity} and remain visibly
unresolved, rather than both being handed the same login and silently
merged into one student.

<<attach the login IDs>>=
def attach_logins(rows, course):
    """Attach each row's student login ID from the course roster."""
    <<index the roster by every identifier it offers>>
    <<look each row up in that index>>
@

A roster we cannot read is a reason to fall back, not to stop: the
analysis of the answers themselves does not need the identity, and a
missing enrolment scope or an expired token should not cost us the
summaries.
So the failure is announced and the rows are left as they came.

<<index the roster by every identifier it offers>>=
by_id, by_sis, by_name = {}, {}, {}
try:
    students = list(course.get_users(enrollment_type=["student"]))
except Exception as error:
    print(f"warning: cannot read the roster of {COURSE}: {error}")
    return
for user in students:
    login = getattr(user, "login_id", None)
    if not login:
        continue
    by_id[str(user.id)] = login
    sis = getattr(user, "sis_user_id", None)
    if sis:
        by_sis[str(sis)] = login
    by_name.setdefault(user.name, []).append(login)
@

A roster entry without a login ID is skipped rather than indexed under
its name: indexing it would make a name that belongs to one usable
student look ambiguous, and we would refuse a lookup we could have
made.

The report's column headings are Canvas's, so we find the two ID
columns by name once and reuse them for every row.
The three lookups are tried in turn and the first that answers wins,
which is also the order of how much we trust them.
A row that none of them answers is left without the column, which is
exactly what [[student_of]] reads as \enquote{fall back}.

<<look each row up in that index>>=
if not rows:
    return
id_column = next((c for c in rows[0] if c.strip().lower() == "id"), None)
sis_column = next((c for c in rows[0]
                   if c.strip().lower() == "sis_id"), None)
for row in rows:
    login = None
    if id_column and row.get(id_column):
        login = by_id.get(str(row[id_column]).strip())
    if not login and sis_column and row.get(sis_column):
        login = by_sis.get(str(row[sis_column]).strip())
    if not login:
        candidates = by_name.get(row.get(STUDENT, ""), [])
        if len(candidates) == 1:
            login = candidates[0]
        elif len(candidates) > 1:
            print(f"warning: several students in {COURSE} are named"
                  f" {row.get(STUDENT)!r} and the report has no usable ID"
                  f" column; falling back to the name, which cannot"
                  f" tell them apart")
    if login:
        row[LOGIN_COLUMN] = login
@

