\subsubsection{Consent}
\label{app:consent-filter}

Consent is asked once, at the head of the background quiz, and covers
all the study's instruments (\cref{ethics}); only the background
report carries the [[Consent]] column, so analysing the debugging
quizzes needs the background report's consent set.
The set is keyed by [[student_of]] like every other join, and
[[None]] is distinct from the empty set on purpose: nobody consenting
must filter everything, while the item being absent means there is
nothing to filter by, which the caller announces rather than guessing
silently in either direction.

<<the consent filter>>=
def consenting(rows):
    """Return the students who answered yes, or None without the column."""
    if not rows:
        return None
    column = column_for(CONSENT, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return None
    return {student_of(row) for row in rows
            if (row[column] or "").strip().lower() in ("yes", "true")}


def filter_consent(rows, students):
    """Keep the rows of the consenting students; None keeps every row."""
    if students is None:
        print("note: no consent answers to filter by; keeping every row")
        return rows
    return [row for row in rows if student_of(row) in students]
@

The distinction between an empty set and no set is the part worth
pinning down with a test:

<<test functions>>=
def test_the_consent_set_holds_exactly_the_yes_answers():
