\subsubsection{Summaries and the coding sheet}

Tabulating a closed item is counting its answer frequencies; we print
one table per item present in the report (the preparation item only
exists in the end quiz).
Every report now carries the score column the reader sums
(\cref{app:reading-the-report}), but only the debugging quizzes grade
their items; the background quiz's are worth nothing, and a
distribution of zeros is not a summary, so the summary is printed only
when something was earned.

<<tabulate the closed items>>=
def tabulate(rows, item):
    """Print the answer frequencies for one closed item."""
    column = column_for(item, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    counts = collections.Counter(row[column] for row in rows)
    print(f"\n{item}")
    for answer, count in counts.most_common():
        print(f"  {answer or '(no answer)'}: {count}")


def summarize_scores(rows):
    """Print the score distribution, if any points were earned."""
    column = column_for(SCORE_COLUMN, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    scores = [float(row[column]) for row in rows if row[column]]
    if any(scores):
        print(f"\nscores: n={len(scores)}"
              f", mean={statistics.mean(scores):.1f}"
              f", median={statistics.median(scores):.1f}"
              f", max={max(scores):.0f}")
@

The model suggests the codes from the two open answers it is shown:
the languages, the rough amount of experience and whether Python is
among the languages from the first, and which of the three patterns of
variation the described steps exhibit from the second.
The prompt defines the patterns briefly; the model must answer in JSON
so that the suggestions land in columns.
The suggestions are exactly that---\cref{analysis} requires a human
coder, and the verified codes go in the bare columns.

<<pre-code the open answers with an LLM>>=
CODING_PROMPT = """\
You are pre-coding survey answers for a study on debugging.
Answer in JSON with keys "languages" (comma-separated list of the
programming languages mentioned), "experience" (roughly how much
programming experience, in the student's own words, e.g. "2 years"),
"python" ("yes", "no" or "unclear"), "contrast", "generalisation",
"fusion" (each 0 or 1), and "rationale" (one sentence).

"languages", "experience" and "python" concern the languages answer.
The pattern codes concern the debugging-exercise answer, in which the
student describes how they would find out why a small program prints
the wrong thing:
"contrast" = compares the failing case against a working one;
"generalisation" = varies other things to see if the problem persists,
keeping the suspected cause fixed;
"fusion" = reconciles the found cause with prior understanding,
varying several things together.

Languages answer: {previous}
Debugging-exercise answer: {exercise}
"""


def llm_precode(rows):
    """Ask the default llm model to suggest codes for each row."""
    import llm

    model = llm.get_model()
    suggestions = []
    for row in rows:
        prompt = CODING_PROMPT.format(
            previous=row.get(
                column_for(CODED_OPEN_ITEMS[0], row.keys()) or "", ""),
            exercise=row.get(
                column_for(CODED_OPEN_ITEMS[1], row.keys()) or "", ""),
        )
        try:
            suggestion = json.loads(model.prompt(prompt).text())
        except (json.JSONDecodeError, Exception):
            suggestion = {}
        suggestions.append(
            {f"suggested_{key}": suggestion.get(key, "")
             for key in CODED_KEYS + ["rationale"]}
        )
    return suggestions
@

The coding sheet holds the open-text answers, the model's suggested
codes when [[--llm]] was given, and the empty columns for the human
coder's verified codes.
The suggested huvudman columns are there whether or not the lookup has
run: the register is a directory of files rather than a service that
may be unavailable, so the sheet has one shape and not two, and a blank
there means \enquote{not looked up yet} rather than \enquote{no column}.
It carries the login ID beside the name, because the sheet is not read
in isolation: its codes are the [[quiz_class]] of [[students.csv]]
(\cref{app:episodes}), which the episode analysis joins against the
questionnaire scores, and that join is on the login ID.
The name stays because the coder is a person, who needs to recognise
whose answer this is when a code has to be discussed.

<<write the coding sheet>>=
def write_coding_sheet(rows, path, suggestions=None):
    """Write open-text answers with coding columns to path."""
    open_columns = {
        item: column_for(item, rows[0].keys()) for item in OPEN_ITEMS
    }
    fields = ([STUDENT, LOGIN_COLUMN] + OPEN_ITEMS + SUGGESTED_HUVUDMAN
              + (SUGGESTED_COLUMNS if suggestions else []) + CODING_COLUMNS)
    with open(path, "w", newline="") as out:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=fields, extrasaction="ignore")
        writer.writeheader()
        for index, row in enumerate(rows):
            record = {STUDENT: row.get(STUDENT, ""),
                      LOGIN_COLUMN: row.get(LOGIN_COLUMN, "")}
            for item, column in open_columns.items():
                record[item] = row.get(column, "") if column else ""
            if suggestions:
                record.update(suggestions[index])
            writer.writerow(record)
@

The sheet is what a namesake pair would spoil quietly, since two rows
under one name look like one student's two attempts, so let us see
both columns come out:

