\subsubsection{Looking the schools up in the register}
\label{app:school-lookup}

The coding sheet now carries the school answers, and this is where they
are settled.
What we want from them---who ran the school---is not a judgement about
the answer but a fact about the school, recorded by Skolverket in
Skolenhetsregistret, and the same answer looked up in the same register
file yields the same huvudman today, next year, and in whatever
replication comes after.
That is why this is a program of its own rather than another prompt for
the model of \cref{app:quiz-analysis}: the model is asked what an
answer \emph{shows}, which is a reading and needs a human to verify it,
whereas the register is asked what \emph{is}, which needs only to be
looked up correctly.
Mixing the two would put a retrieved fact and a suggested reading in
the same column and make the sheet stop distinguishing them.

The coder still verifies, and the pair of columns of
\cref{app:quiz-analysis} is what makes room for that: the register can
only be as right as the answer it is given, and a student who misremembers
the name, writes the school next door, or names a school that is not a
school unit at all produces a lookup that is deterministic and wrong.
The lookup writes only into the suggested column, and never touches the
verified one, so re-running it after the coder has started costs
nothing.

\paragraph{Why the register of that year, and why a mirror of it}

A register answers about a moment, and the moment the current
Skolenhetsregistret answers about is today.
The students went to school between six and twelve years ago, and
school names drift: a fifth of the grundskola units and better than a
third of the gymnasium units on record for läsår 2015/16 go by a
different name now.
Al-Azharskolan is Jacobiskolan today, under a different stiftelse.
Looked up in today's register, the name a student writes for a school
that has since been renamed matches nothing---or, worse, matches the
school that took the name over, and returns a huvudman that was never
theirs.
The huvudmannatyp itself hardly ever moves (about one grundskola unit
in a thousand has changed type since 2013), so the year we look in is
not about the type changing under the school; it is about finding the
school at all.

So we look in the register of the year the student was there, which
means having the register of that year.
Skolverket's legacy SIRIS export API still serves one CSV per läsår,
but it is undocumented, its web interface is already decommissioned,
and nothing about it promises to answer tomorrow.
The files under [[skolenheter/]] are therefore mirrored into the
repository and committed with it: they are the record, and the
Makefile's [[skolenheter]] and [[skolenheter-refresh]] targets are a
convenience for fetching them again, not the source of truth.

Which year belongs to which stage follows from the cohort.
A student starting the course in 2026 straight from gymnasiet was born
around 2007, began årskurs~1 in the autumn of 2014, and moved up a
stage every three years from there.
We take the year each stage \emph{began}, since that is the school the
student is likeliest to name when asked where they went in lågstadiet,
and a student who moved mid-stage is a case for the coder either way.
The keys are the stage names of \cref{app:quiz-analysis}; a stage
missing here is a [[KeyError]] the moment the registers load, which is
as early as we could want to hear about it.

<<the school stages and their register years>>=
REGISTER_DIRECTORY = "skolenheter"
STAGE_REGISTERS = {
    "lågstadiet": ("grundskola", 2014),
    "mellanstadiet": ("grundskola", 2017),
    "högstadiet": ("grundskola", 2020),
    "gymnasiet": ("gymnasieskola", 2023),
}
@

Those years fit a student of the nominal age, and the cohort will not
consist only of those.
A student who took a gap year, a basår, or a second gymnasium
programme was at each stage earlier than the table says, and one who
started school late was there later.
The mirror spans läsår 2010--2022 for grundskola and 2015--2024 for
gymnasieskola so that such a student still lands in a mirrored year,
and the loader falls back to the nearest one it has rather than
failing.
The cost of being a year or two off is small for the same reason the
year matters at all: it is the name that drifts, not the type.

