\paragraph{Meeting the student's spelling}

Two strings that name the same school are rarely the same string.
The student types in a browser, and a browser on one operating system
may send [[å]] as one code point and on another as an [[a]] followed by
a combining ring, which compare unequal; composing both to the same
normal form settles that before anything else does.
Case and stray whitespace go the same way, and so does the punctuation
that trails an answer someone ended with a full stop.

<<normalise what students and the register write differently>>=
def normalize(text):
    """Return text in the form the register indexes are keyed on."""
    text = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", text or "")
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip(" .,;:-").casefold()
@

What remains is a difference of substance rather than spelling: the
register names units, and a student names a school.
A school with more than a few hundred pupils is usually several units
split by the grades they teach, and the register writes the split into
the name---\enquote{Lendahlsskolan F-3} and \enquote{Lendahlsskolan
4-6}, \enquote{Kyrkebyskolan åk 7-9}, \enquote{Nossebro skola år
0-5}---while the student writes \enquote{Lendahlsskolan}.
This is not a rare shape: 15.7\% of the grundskola unit names across
the mirrored years end in such a span, against three names in 12,944
for gymnasieskola, which is why the problem shows up at the stages
where the schools are big and the students are small.

Cutting the span off and matching the base name merges the siblings,
and that is only sound if siblings agree about their huvudman.
Across all 23 mirrored files---75,232 unit-years---they always do, so
the merge never has to choose (which is checked below rather than
trusted).
The pattern stretches a little to cover how the register writes the
span, since \enquote{åk}, \enquote{år} and neither all occur in front
of one, and it is anchored at the end so that a digit that is part of a
name survives.
The separator is the plain hyphen and only that: of the 11,282 dashes
in the mirrored school names, every one is U+002D, so admitting the
typographic dashes would widen the pattern against nothing.

<<normalise what students and the register write differently>>=
GRADE_SPAN = re.compile(
    r"\s+(?:åk|år)?\s*(?:f|\d{1,2})\s*-\s*(?:åk|år)?\s*\d{1,2}\s*$",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)


def normalize_school(name):
    """Return the base name of a school, without its grade span."""
    return normalize(GRADE_SPAN.sub("", (name or "").strip()))
@

The grade span is not the register's only way of writing a split into
the name.
Where a school is divided some other way---Stockholm's large municipal
gymnasier above all---the register appends the unit's own code, and
\enquote{Blackebergs gymnasium} appears three times over as
\enquote{Blackebergs gymnasium 52791537} and its two siblings.
This one we can remove exactly rather than recognise by shape, because
the code we would be stripping is in the row we are stripping it from,
which is why it is done here on the register's side and not in
[[normalize_school]]: a student never writes a unit code, so the
answer's side has nothing to strip.
It is 0.5\% of the mirrored unit names in all---but 25 of the roughly
1,300 gymnasium units of läsår 2023/24, and they are Stockholm's
municipal ones, so leaving them unmatched would thin out exactly the
group the analysis contrasts the friskolor against.

<<normalise what students and the register write differently>>=
def unit_name(row):
    """Return a unit's school name without its own code appended.

    The register disambiguates the units of one school by adding the
    unit code to the name; students write the name alone.
    """
    name = cell(row, NAME_COLUMN)
    code = cell(row, UNIT_COLUMN)
    if code and name.endswith(" " + code):
        name = name[: -len(code)].strip()
    return name
@

The municipality needs less, and needs it in the other direction.
Students write \enquote{Gävle kommun} or \enquote{Stockholms stad}
where the register writes \enquote{Gävle} and \enquote{Stockholm}, so
the trailing word comes off.
That leaves the genitive [[s]] of \enquote{Stockholms stad} behind, and
stemming it away is exactly what we must not do---it would turn
\enquote{Höganäs} into \enquote{Höganä}.
The register side is where the ambiguity is absent, so that is the side
that gets the extra key: below, each municipality is indexed under its
own name and under that name with an [[s]], which is safe because no
Swedish municipality's name is another's plus an [[s]].

<<normalise what students and the register write differently>>=
KOMMUN_SUFFIXES = ("kommun", "stad")


def normalize_kommun(name):
    """Return a municipality name without a kommun or stad suffix."""
    words = normalize(name).split()
    if len(words) > 1 and words[-1] in KOMMUN_SUFFIXES:
        words = words[:-1]
    return " ".join(words)
@

