\paragraph{The index}

An index maps the pair we can form from an answer---base name and
municipality---to the huvudman.
The pair is what makes the lookup possible at all: school names repeat
across the country, and the name alone is ambiguous for about one
grundskola unit in eleven, whereas the pair is unique.
It stays unique after the grade spans come off, since collapsing
siblings only ever merges units of one school in one municipality.

Both of the register's disambiguators are gone by the time the key is
built, so a group is every unit of one school in one municipality
however that school was split.
The merge is where a contradiction would hide, so it is checked rather
than assumed: across the mirror it joins 3,076 such groups, and not one
of them disagrees about its huvudman.
A group whose units disagree about the huvudman is stored as [[None]],
which the lookup reports as unsettled instead of picking one; and it
says so on the way past, because a group like that means either the
register or our pattern has changed, and both are worth knowing about.

