\paragraph{Reading the answer}

The item asks for the name and the municipality on one line, separated
by a comma, and it is the \emph{rightmost} comma that separates them: a
school called \enquote{Sjölins Gymnasium, Södermalm} keeps its comma,
and splitting on the first one would hand us a municipality called
\enquote{Södermalm}.
An answer without a comma is all name and no place, which is what the
lookup then has to work with.

<<read one school answer>>=
def parse_answer(answer):
    """Split a school answer into its school and its place.

    The rightmost comma separates them, so a school whose own name
    contains one survives; an answer without a comma is all school.
    """
    text = " ".join((answer or "").split())
    school, _, place = text.rpartition(",")
    return (school.strip(), place.strip()) if school else (text, "")
@

The stems tell students who went to school outside Sweden to write the
country where the municipality would go, and those answers have no
huvudman to find---the register covers Sweden.
Recognising them is the one place this program uses a list it cannot
finish, and it is arranged so that being unfinished cannot do harm.
A place we recognise as a country is coded [[abroad]]; a place we do
not recognise is [[unknown]] and appears in the run's summary, which is
where the coder is looking anyway.
So the list only ever saves the coder work---it can turn an
[[unknown]] into an [[abroad]], never a school into a country.
\enquote{Sverige} is deliberately absent for the same reason: a student
who writes it has given us no municipality, and that is a case for the
coder rather than one to guess at.

