\paragraph{Saying what was and was not settled}

The summary is the handover to the coder.
It says which register year each stage was read from, so that a stage
answered from a file four years off is visible rather than implied;
it counts the outcomes; and it prints the unmatched answers themselves.

Those are the ones for the bitemporal fallback, which is a manual
route and stays one.
Skolenhetsregistret's current API keeps units that have closed and
answers as of a date: a [[GET]] of
[[/skolenhetsregistret/v2/school-units/<code>]] with
[[?search_date=YYYY-MM-DD]] returns the unit as it stood then, back to
2013-10-01.
It has no name search, though, so it can only be reached from a unit
code---which is precisely what an unmatched answer does not give us.
Automating it would mean guessing the code we failed to find, so the
program stops at naming the answers a person should go and look up.

<<report what the lookup settled>>=
OUTCOMES = ("matched", "abroad", "ambiguous", "unmatched", "blank")


def report(outcomes, unmatched, registers):
    """Print what the lookup settled, and what it left for the coder."""
    for stage in SCHOOL_STAGES:
        form, _ = STAGE_REGISTERS[stage]
        counts = ", ".join(
            f"{outcomes[(stage, outcome)]} {outcome}"
            for outcome in OUTCOMES if outcomes[(stage, outcome)]
        )
        print(f"{stage} (from {form} {registers[stage][1]}):"
              f" {counts or 'nothing to look up'}")
    if not unmatched:
        return
    print("\nnot found in the register; look these up by hand:")
    for (stage, answer), count in unmatched.most_common():
        print(f"  {stage}: {answer!r}" + (f" (x{count})" if count > 1 else ""))
@

\paragraph{Running it}

The sheet is rewritten in place by default, because the suggested
columns are the only ones written and the coder's work sits in the
others; an [[--output]] is there for anyone who would rather keep the
original.
A sheet from before these columns existed is filled all the same---the
columns are appended where they are missing---so an early coding run is
not lost by adding the lookup to it.

<<the lookup main>>=
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--sheet", default="quiz-coding-sheet.csv",
                        help="the coding sheet to fill in")
    parser.add_argument("--registers", default=REGISTER_DIRECTORY,
                        help="directory of mirrored register exports")
    parser.add_argument("--output",
                        help="write here instead of over the sheet")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    with open(args.sheet, newline="") as sheet:
        reader = csv.DictReader(sheet)
        rows, fields = list(reader), list(reader.fieldnames or [])
    if not rows:
        sys.exit(f"{args.sheet} has no rows")

    registers = load_registers(args.registers)
    outcomes, unmatched = fill_sheet(rows, registers)
    fields += [column for column in SUGGESTED_HUVUDMAN
               if column not in fields]
    with open(args.output or args.sheet, "w", newline="") as out:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=fields, extrasaction="ignore")
        writer.writeheader()
        writer.writerows(rows)
    report(outcomes, unmatched, registers)
@
